\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Example}
\title{One marked time link on two circles}
\date{2026-11-26}

\p{Take the one-direction real flip #{\varepsilon_t=X} and
grading #{\Gamma=Z} from the sign operator in \ref{fdip-00LB}.
Take #{c} to conjugate coefficients in this real spin basis.
Let the fiber have orthonormal basis #{v_0,v_1,v_2}. Write #{g_F v_j=v_{j+1}}
with indices modulo three and take #{c_F=g_F\kappa_0}, where
#{\kappa_0} conjugates coefficients in this basis. The cycle
is real, so #{c_F^2=g_F^2} is nonscalar. Since the spin factor
#{ZX} is real and squares to minus the identity,
\ref{fdip-0181} gives #{S_t^2=-\bbI_S\otimes g_F}:
indeed #{c_F^{-2}=g_F^{-2}=g_F}.}

\p{On a time circle of period #{N_t=3} or #{4}, mark only
the positive link at zero with #{U_t(0)=\operatorname{diag}
(\ui,1,1)}. The other links are identities. The reflected
positive link can be marked only at #{x=N_t-1}, since its
source in the original assignment is #{-x-1=0}. On that
link the formula in \ref{fdip-0182} gives
##{b_F(U_t(0)^{-1})
 =g_F^{-1}U_t(0)g_F=\operatorname{diag}(1,1,\ui).}
Conjugating instead by #{c_F} on the left would put the phase
on #{v_1} instead of #{v_2}. Both the inverse fiber map and
the one-step displacement are visible.}

\p{At site zero the reflected forward shift therefore reads the
value at #{N_t-1} with the displayed coefficient. This is the
inverse shift for the reflected connection. At every other
site the coefficient is the identity. The centered difference
changes sign, the spin flip changes sign, and the sum of the
two shifts is preserved, giving the centered, Laplace and
Wilson identities on each circle. With every link trivial
the same shift interchange holds at every site with identity
coefficient. Period #{3} fixes only time zero, and period
#{4} fixes times zero and two.}
